 - `extract ARCHIVE DEST [--overwrite]` extracts files and directories into
   `DEST` (created if needed), keeping their relative paths and contents, then
   prints `extracted N files` — the number of regular files written.
+- `create ARCHIVE PATH... [--overwrite]` writes a new archive holding each
+  `PATH` — a file or directory relative to the current directory, a directory
+  bringing everything inside it — then prints `created ARCHIVE with N files`,
+  the number of regular files added.
 
 Plain `.tar` and gzip-compressed `.tar.gz` / `.tgz` archives both work
-(`.tar.bz2` and `.tar.xz` are detected too, at no extra cost).
+(`.tar.bz2` and `.tar.xz` are detected too when reading, at no extra cost).
+`create` writes gzip when `ARCHIVE` ends in `.tar.gz` or `.tgz`, and a plain
+tar archive otherwise.
 
 ## Exit status
 
 | Code | Meaning |
 | ---- | ------- |
 | 0 | success |
-| 1 | the destination could not be written (e.g. `DEST` exists as a file) |
-| 2 | `ARCHIVE` does not exist, is unreadable, or is not a valid tar archive |
-| 3 | extraction would overwrite an existing file and `--overwrite` was not given |
+| 1 | an I/O failure while reading the sources or writing the output |
+| 2 | `ARCHIVE` does not exist, is unreadable, or is not a valid tar archive; or a `PATH` given to `create` does not exist |
+| 3 | the command would overwrite something and `--overwrite` was not given |
 
 Usage errors (unknown command, missing argument) exit 2 via `argparse`.
 
 ## Behaviour worth knowing
 
+### Creating
+
+- **A failed `create` leaves no archive.** Every `PATH` is checked before any
+  writing starts, so a missing one fails with exit 2 and nothing on disk. The
+  archive is then built under a temporary name beside its destination and moved
+  into place only once complete, so an interrupted run leaves neither a
+  half-written archive nor a destroyed `--overwrite` target.
+- **Member names are the relative paths given**, normalised: `./photos/` and
+  `photos` both store `photos`, `photos/a.jpg`, … A directory also gets a member
+  of its own, so empty directories survive a `create`/`extract` round trip.
+  Entries are added in sorted order, so the same tree produces the same archive.
+- **Only relative paths inside the current directory are accepted.** An absolute
+  `PATH`, or one starting with `..`, is refused (exit 2): it has no relative name
+  to be stored under. Repeating a path (or naming a file already covered by a
+  directory) adds it once.
+- **The archive is never added to itself**, so `create photos/inside.tar photos`
+  works and contains only the photos.
+- **A `PATH` naming a symlink is followed**, since it was asked for by name;
+  symlinks found inside a directory are stored as symlinks, like `tar` does.
+  Neither counts towards the `N files` total, which counts regular files.
+  Sockets and other members tar cannot represent are skipped.
+- **An existing `ARCHIVE` is an error** (exit 3) unless `--overwrite` is given.
+  A directory in `ARCHIVE`'s place is always an error, even with `--overwrite`.
+  The parent directory of `ARCHIVE` must already exist.
+
+### Extracting
+
 - **Nothing is written when extraction would clobber.** Conflicts are detected
   in a pass over the whole archive before the first byte is written, so a
   failing extraction leaves the destination untouched rather than half-filled.
